# J.A.R.V.I.S AI Operating System — environment configuration
#
# None of these are required to run the app. With nothing set, every screen
# runs in DEMO / SIMULATION mode: chat gets scripted responses, telemetry
# and diagnostics are locally simulated, and voice uses the browser's
# built-in speech APIs. Set any one AI provider below to go live for chat.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in only what you need.
# Never commit .env.local — it's already in .gitignore.

# --- AI provider (pick at most one; checked in this priority order) ---

# OpenRouter (https://openrouter.ai) — checked first.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5

# Groq (https://groq.com) — checked second.
GROQ_API_KEY=
GROQ_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile

# Any OpenAI-compatible /chat/completions endpoint — checked third.
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_API_KEY=
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_BASE_URL=
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# --- n8n workflow integration ---
# Server-only. Used by both /api/chat (as a last-resort provider, if no AI
# key above is set) and /api/n8n (a dedicated forwarding endpoint). This
# value is never sent to the browser — the client only ever calls the
# internal Next.js API routes.
N8N_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Additional named workflows the n8n_workflow tool and automation registry
# can trigger, e.g. N8N_WORKFLOW_MORNING_ROUTINE_URL registers a
# "morning-routine" workflow. Optional — only N8N_WEBHOOK_URL is required
# for basic automation.
# N8N_WORKFLOW_<NAME>_URL=

# Only needed for the get_workflow_status tool (checking a triggered run's
# progress) — a webhook alone can't be polled, this needs n8n's REST API.
# N8N_BASE_URL=
# N8N_API_KEY=

# --- Voice: speech-to-text / text-to-speech (all optional) ---
# The app already works with zero configuration via the browser's built-in
# SpeechRecognition and SpeechSynthesis APIs (Settings → Voice → "Browser").
# Selecting a server provider below without its key set is safe: the app
# detects the 501 and falls back to the browser provider automatically,
# with a visible toast explaining why.

# Whisper STT + OpenAI TTS — both reuse OPENAI_API_KEY (the same key used
# for embeddings in src/lib/memory/embeddingsServer.ts).
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_TTS_VOICE=alloy

# AssemblyAI STT — Phase 5's primary production STT provider. Also enables
# real automatic language detection (en/ur/hi) as one signal feeding the
# app's own Roman-Urdu/Hinglish heuristic layer (src/lib/voice/language/).
# ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY=

# ElevenLabs TTS.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=21m00Tcm4TlvDq8ikWAM

# Azure AI Speech — Phase 5's primary production TTS provider, with
# per-language neural voice selection (src/lib/voice/tts/azure.ts). Server-
# side only; never exposed to the browser under any NEXT_PUBLIC_ variant.
# AZURE_SPEECH_KEY=
# AZURE_SPEECH_REGION=centralindia
# AZURE_SPEECH_ENDPOINT=https://centralindia.api.cognitive.microsoft.com/
# Optional — a specific neural voice overriding the automatic per-language
# selection below (see lib/voice/tts/voiceProfiles.ts for the defaults).
# AZURE_SPEECH_VOICE=

# Which STT/TTS provider Settings defaults new sessions to when nothing is
# persisted yet — still just a default, always changeable in Settings →
# Voice & Language, and both still fall back automatically if unconfigured.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is correct (not a mistake) here specifically: this carries
# only a provider *name* (never a key/secret), and Next.js only inlines
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars into the client bundle at build time — which is what
# lets the client-side settings default actually read it. Every other
# voice env var above stays server-only.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VOICE_STT_PROVIDER=assemblyai
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VOICE_TTS_PROVIDER=azure

# --- Rate limiting (src/lib/security/rateLimit.ts) ---
# Every route that reaches an LLM/voice/research provider or triggers a
# real external workflow (/api/chat, /api/reasoning, /api/voice/*,
# /api/research, /api/automation, /api/tools/weather, /api/n8n) is capped
# per client (best-effort IP, from the X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP proxy
# headers) using an in-memory fixed-window limiter. No configuration is
# needed for this — it works out of the box on a single instance.
#
# PRODUCTION REQUIREMENT: this in-memory limiter's state lives in one
# process and resets on every deploy or restart, and is NOT shared across
# multiple instances of a horizontally-scaled deployment (serverless,
# multiple containers) — a client can get a fresh limit just by landing on
# a different instance. For real production traffic, swap the
# InMemoryRateLimiter instances in rateLimit.ts for a shared backend (e.g.
# Upstash Redis's @upstash/ratelimit sliding-window limiter) behind the
# same RateLimiter interface — no call site needs to change.
